  "summary": "Exact denominator clearing yields rigorous normalized gap bounds for 10 n=10 targets, but even the strongest has log10 lower bound -2057.6; square-freeness alone is far too weak to justify efficient phase estimation.",
  "theorem_contract": {
    "bound": "If M=m(m-1)/2, the raw minimum gap is at least B^{-(m-1)} 4^{-(M-1)}; LCU normalization contributes another 1/2.",
    "discriminant": "For square-free B chi_H in Z[x], the nonzero integer discriminant has magnitude at least one.",
    "interpretation": "This is a rigorous existence bound, not evidence that the actual gaps are this small. Its role is to show that finite square-freeness does not establish efficient precision.",
    "newton_denominators": "Newton identities imply the kth characteristic coefficient has denominator dividing k! Q^k. Thus B=m! Q^m clears the entire monic characteristic polynomial.",
    "root_range": "H is a sum of two Hermitian unitary averages, so every root lies in [-2,2] and every non-minimal pair gap is at most four.",
    "trace_denominators": "For degrees i<=m, Tr(P_T H^i) has denominator dividing D_P D_H^m, where D_P is the exact YJM Lagrange-projector denominator and D_H clears both orbit averages."
  }
